1. What is List in Java?
List is an ordered collection in the Java Collections Framework.

Key Characteristics:
Maintains insertion order
Allows duplicate elements
Supports index-based access
Allows null values (implementation dependent)

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

Interview one-liner
“List is an ordered collection that allows duplicates and index-based access.”

2. List Hierarchy (Must-Know)
Iterable
 └── Collection
      └── List
           ├── ArrayList
           ├── LinkedList
           ├── Vector
                └── Stack

3. Common List Implementations
| Implementation | Data Structure     | Thread-Safe | Best Use Case          |
| -------------- | ------------------ | ----------- | ---------------------- |
| ArrayList      | Dynamic array      | ❌           | Fast read              |
| LinkedList     | Doubly linked list | ❌           | Frequent insert/delete |
| Vector         | Dynamic array      | ✅           | Legacy                 |
| Stack          | LIFO               | ✅           | Legacy stack           |

4. ArrayList (🔥 Most Used)
Example
  import java.util.*;
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
          list.add("Java");
          list.add("Spring");
          list.add("Java");
  
          System.out.println(list);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  [Java, Spring, Java]

Key Points:
Fast random access → O(1)
Slow insertion/removal in middle → O(n)
Not synchronized

5. LinkedList
Example
  import java.util.*;
  
  public class Test {
      public static void main(String[] args) {
          List<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();
          list.add(10);
          list.add(20);
          list.add(30);
  
          list.remove(1);
          System.out.println(list);
      }
  }
  
  Output
  [10, 30]

Key Points:
  Fast insert/delete → O(1)
  Slow access → O(n)
  Implements Deque (can be used as Queue)

6. Vector (Legacy)
  Vector<Integer> v = new Vector<>();
  v.add(1);
  v.add(2);
  System.out.println(v);
  
  Output
  [1, 2]

Thread-safe (synchronized)
Performance overhead
Rarely used now

7. Stack (Legacy – LIFO)
  Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
  stack.push(10);
  stack.push(20);
  stack.pop();
  System.out.println(stack);
  
  Output
  [10]


👉 Prefer Deque / ArrayDeque instead of Stack.

